<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attack on Pearl Harbor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pearl Harbor · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The attack on Pearl Harbor was the surprise attack by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>'s naval air forces on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Pacific Fleet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> on 7 December 1941. It aimed to cripple American naval power so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> could seize resources in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. It brought the United States into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, a decision that led to Japan's eventual defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor.jpg" alt="Attack on Pearl Harbor" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imperial Japanese Navy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attack_on_Pearl_Harbor_Japanese_planes_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan imported roughly 80 per cent of its oil from the United States, so the American oil embargo of August 1941 pushed Tokyo towards attacking <b>Pearl Harbor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Allied Powers in the First World War and committed 10,000 soldiers for Europe; in the Second World War it declared neutrality, signed a Lend-Lease agreement for US$7.2 million of equipment in November 1941, of which little arrived, and declared war on the Axis after <b>Pearl Harbor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Front in 1918, and in the Second World War, which the United States entered after the Japanese attack on <b>Pearl Harbor</b> on 7 December 1941, its industry and forces fought in Europe and the Pacific, and the war ended after atomic bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Pearl Harbor</b> it was heard on British, American and Soviet radio, opened Frank Capra's film The Battle of China (1944) and was used by Allied broadcasts to stand for China.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Pacific War: Imperial General Headquarters announcements that reported the annihilation of units fighting to the last always opened with it, though it was also played for the success of the attack on <b>Pearl Harbor</b>; other victories were announced with songs such as &quot;Battōtai&quot; and the &quot;Warship March&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a></li><li><blockquote>Four days after <b>Pearl Harbor</b>, Hitler and Mussolini declared war on the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
